==Old English==

===Etymology===
(Closely related to [[leod|lēod]] and [[leode|lēode]]).  From {{proto|gem-pro|leudanan|lang=ang}}, from Indo-European ''*leudh-'' ‘to grow, come up’.  Cognates include Old Saxon [[liodan]], Old High German [[liotan]], Gothic [[𐌻𐌹𐌿𐌳𐌰𐌽]].  The Indo-European root is also the source of Albanian {{term|lind||to be born, to spring|lang=sq}} < {{term|lej||to generate, produce, give birth to|lang=sq}}, Greek {{l|grc|ἐλεύθερος}} ‘free, freeman’, Latin [[liber]] ‘free’, Old Irish [[luss]] ‘plant’.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈleːodan/|lang=ang}}

===Verb===
{{ang-verb|type=strong|class=II|head=lēodan}}

# to [[grow]]
#: ''Of ðam twige '''ludon''' reðe wæstme'': from that branch sprang dire fruits.  (Cædmon's ''Metrical Paraphrase'')